WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.626 align:start size:3% position:97% line:7%
♪

00:00:01.626 --> 00:00:05.588
We are in basement of Jonathan
Edwards College at the JE Press.

00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:08.883
JE Press has been around
since about 1936.

00:00:09.008 --> 00:00:12.470
So we are standing
in one of the last remaining

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:14.973
undergraduate
print shops on campus.

00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:18.268
[relaxed groove music]

00:00:19.894 --> 00:00:21.771 line:83%
The unique feature
of this particular space

00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:24.023 line:83%
is that we're practicing
typography, essentially,

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:26.693 line:83%
as Gutenberg envisioned it,
about 600 years ago.

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.278
What we are specifically doing
is printing

00:00:28.278 --> 00:00:29.571
from handset metal type.

00:00:29.571 --> 00:00:32.782
And up until about 1950,
letterpress was just the way

00:00:32.782 --> 00:00:33.992
most things got printed.

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:36.703 line:83%
Almost every college,
had its own printing press

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:39.998 line:83%
and somewhere in the college
that was sort of a service

00:00:39.998 --> 00:00:41.958 line:83%
for students to use
so that they could

00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:45.754
print like programs for concerts
or like posters for events

00:00:45.754 --> 00:00:47.297
or their own stationery

00:00:47.297 --> 00:00:49.174
or any really
anything that they wanted.

00:00:49.174 --> 00:00:51.634
Jesse:
You know, letterpress I think

00:00:49.174 --> 00:00:51.634 size:75% position:38% line:78%
[sound of machine pedal]

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:55.388
it really is in service
of the undergraduate experience.

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:57.766
[sound of rollers]

00:00:57.766 --> 00:01:00.185
It’s not just here for mere
amusement sake like it,

00:01:00.185 --> 00:01:01.186
it does provide

00:01:01.186 --> 00:01:04.022
a practical function
within the life of the college,

00:01:04.022 --> 00:01:07.984
it seems to be a more enriched
form of gift giving.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.194
You know,
this is something that I made

00:01:09.194 --> 00:01:10.028
and I'm sharing it with you.

00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:13.031
I think that really appeals
to people.

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:15.992
[relaxed music]

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:20.830 line:83%
The first step of letterpress
printing anything that’s words

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:22.040 line:85%
always starts with type.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.875 line:85%
This is a type case.

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.294
This is 12 point Centaur.

00:01:26.294 --> 00:01:28.880
and each of these cases
contains one font

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.841
and a font is one size and style
of a typeface.

00:01:31.841 --> 00:01:34.427
[squeak of drawers closing]

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:38.264
We have about
385 typecases

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.307
in here in the cabinets

00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:41.810
which would be 385
different fonts.

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:44.813
The first step to using type
is composing

00:01:44.813 --> 00:01:47.023
and this is a composing stick

00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:49.442
and it’s got this adjustable
knee here

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:53.363
so you can adjust how
wide your line length is

00:01:53.363 --> 00:01:54.364
or your measure.

00:01:54.364 --> 00:01:57.075
Say I want to print the line
“Printing Time”

00:01:57.158 --> 00:01:58.660
exclamation point.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.537
I’m starting with a capital P

00:02:00.537 --> 00:02:04.541
i know where that is and
these go upside down

00:02:04.541 --> 00:02:08.336
left to right, and they line up
with these notches on the top.

00:02:09.087 --> 00:02:10.547
Every character is upside down

00:02:10.547 --> 00:02:12.590
but it reads left to right
like normal.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.801
This is the process of
setting type.

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:19.556 size:75% position:38% line:50%
[sound of metal sliding]

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:19.556
Here is another example of
a set page of type.

00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:23.768
This is a little bit fancy
I did this for Valentine’s Day.

00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:26.187
So this is our Vandercook SP-20

00:02:26.187 --> 00:02:29.148
Now we can ink it up,
take an ink knife

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.027
and we’re just gonna
apply it on

00:02:33.027 --> 00:02:35.989 align:start size:3% position:97% line:7%
♪

00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:37.615
Now is the fun part!

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:38.616
We can turn it on.

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:41.327
[whur of machine operating]

00:02:41.452 --> 00:02:44.414
Let this down,
and then let down the rollers

00:02:45.123 --> 00:02:47.792
I’m going to use two sheets
of news print for thickness

00:02:47.792 --> 00:02:49.085
because it’s
pretty thin

00:02:49.085 --> 00:02:51.045
but first, we’ll ink the cut.

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:54.007
[click and sound of rollers]

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:00.513
[click]

00:03:03.057 --> 00:03:03.558
[click]

00:03:03.558 --> 00:03:05.059
Set it to print,

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:06.561
line the paper up,

00:03:06.561 --> 00:03:07.604
[click]

00:03:07.604 --> 00:03:10.189
and you roll it

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:13.151
and the grippers release
at the very end of the bed.

00:03:13.943 --> 00:03:16.905
[relaxed synth beat]

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:21.409
The era of letterpress
really wasn't that long ago

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:23.077
and I think allowing students

00:03:23.077 --> 00:03:26.080
to participate in it
helps collapsed time in a way

00:03:26.289 --> 00:03:28.708
and connects them
to a deeper tradition

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:31.461
[laughing]

00:03:31.461 --> 00:03:34.088
Annli: I was a first year
in Pierson,

00:03:34.088 --> 00:03:37.008
Yeah! I just was curious
about it and I signed up.

00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:40.553
I just kept taking the classes
and I kept just like showing up

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:43.514
and it sort of became
my kind of like

00:03:43.514 --> 00:03:46.643
my evening ritual
throughout all of first year

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:50.146 align:start size:56% position:44% line:28%
There we go! Nice!

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:50.146 align:start position:0% line:78%
<i>Nice it’s beautiful!</i>

00:03:50.146 --> 00:03:51.606
The quality of the work that
people put out

00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:53.441
after only a few
months of training

00:03:53.441 --> 00:03:55.693
is really impressive.

00:03:55.693 --> 00:03:58.696
I think that the
constraints of it

00:03:58.696 --> 00:04:00.531
being limited by size

00:04:00.531 --> 00:04:03.159
and the number of sorts
we have in a type case

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:04.243
and time

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:08.414
creates such a fertile breeding
ground for people’s creativity

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:09.666
even if they don’t
consider theirself

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:11.334
a particularly creative person

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:13.419
being forced to work in
these constraints

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.964
produces incredibly
imaginative stuff.

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:17.131
Jesse:
Letterpress is in a way,

00:04:17.131 --> 00:04:20.134
it kind of coordinates the hand,
the head and the heart

00:04:20.134 --> 00:04:22.428
in a way that other disciplines
can't really

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:23.805
Yale is a place
that you would come

00:04:23.805 --> 00:04:26.849
to be challenged intellectually
and across various

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:28.267
other academic disciplines.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.521
The way that letterpress
forces students

00:04:31.521 --> 00:04:34.816
to participate in the craft
and really slows them down

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:37.819
but gives them the agency
to pursue their own

00:04:37.902 --> 00:04:39.862
creative ambitions.

00:04:39.862 --> 00:04:42.740
I just think that's a
a priceless opportunity

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:43.866
for the right kind of student.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.161 align:start size:6% position:94% line:7%
♪♪

